{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-j1-reviewer-fable-44","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:e38216a09d49fd3302f4","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"The report's selected result — a pooled two-sample Student t-test on all 48 coupon rows yielding t(46) = 23.465, p < 0.0001 — enters each declared independent unit (plate_id, 12 plates) four times into a procedure that assumes row independence. Because build_orientation is constant within a plate and the data show strong plate-level clustering (within-plate coupon range ~6 MPa versus between-plate mean spread ~16 MPa within an orientation), the effective independent sample size is 12 plates (df = 10), not 48 coupons (df = 46). The stated t-statistic, degrees of freedom, and p-value therefore overstate the evidence as reported. This finding is bounded to the validity of the stated inferential result: the direction and magnitude of the mean difference (40 MPa, edge > flat) are unaffected, and a correct plate-level t-test (t(10) ≈ 12.25) would still be significant at p < 0.0001.","root_cause":"The workflow extracts tensile_strength_mpa from every coupon row per orientation (workflow/analysis.py uts_by_orientation) and passes these 24-per-group row-level values directly to scipy.stats.ttest_ind with df = n1 + n2 - 2 = 46, without any aggregation to the declared independent unit plate_id. Each of the 12 plates contributes four correlated coupon measurements to a test that treats every entry as an independent observation, inflating the effective sample size fourfold and the degrees of freedom from 10 to 46.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description declares plate_id as the independent-unit column and states each plate label appears four times (one row per coupon).","observed_value":"Independent unit = plate_id; 4 rows per plate","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":21,"end_line":22,"quoted_text":"One row is: one tensile coupon cut from a build plate and pulled to failure\nIndependent unit column: plate_id"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":13,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"- plate_id: label of the build plate the coupon was cut from. Each plate label\n  appears four times, once per coupon cut from that plate."}]},{"description":"The treatment variable build_orientation is a plate-level property, identical for all four coupons from a plate, so orientation comparisons have only 12 independent units (6 per group).","observed_value":"build_orientation constant within plate; 6 flat plates, 6 edge plates","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":15,"end_line":17,"quoted_text":"- build_orientation: how the plate was oriented during printing, either flat\n  or edge. The value is a property of the plate, so it is the same for all\n  four coupons from a plate."},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":4,"end_line":6,"quoted_text":"study. Twelve metal build plates were produced on the same laser powder-bed\nfusion machine: six plates were printed lying flat and six were printed on\nedge."}]},{"description":"The workflow builds per-orientation lists from every coupon row (no aggregation by plate_id) and runs a row-independent pooled t-test on them with df = 24 + 24 - 2 = 46.","observed_value":"ttest_ind applied to 24 row-level values per group; df = 46","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":24,"end_line":29,"quoted_text":"def uts_by_orientation(rows, orientation):\n    return [\n        float(row[\"tensile_strength_mpa\"])\n        for row in rows\n        if row[\"build_orientation\"] == orientation\n    ]"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":49,"end_line":50,"quoted_text":"outcome = stats.ttest_ind(edge, flat, equal_var=True)\n    df = len(edge) + len(flat) - 2"}]},{"description":"The report's selected result explicitly claims the row-level test as its statistical conclusion, with 48 coupon rows and t(46), and states that each coupon row contributes one observation to the test.","observed_value":"t(46) = 23.465, p < 0.0001 on 48 coupon rows","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":23,"end_line":23,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Two-sample Student t-test (pooled variance) on 48 coupon rows: edge-built coupons averaged 340.500 MPa (SD 6.058) versus 300.500 MPa (SD 5.748) for flat-built coupons, a mean difference of 40.000 MPa, t(46) = 23.465, p < 0.0001."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":19,"end_line":21,"quoted_text":"A two-sample Student t-test with pooled variance compares the ultimate tensile\nstrength of edge-built coupons with that of flat-built coupons. Each coupon row\nin data/input.csv contributes one observation to the test."}]},{"description":"The raw data show marked plate-level clustering: the four coupons from one plate span only ~6 MPa (e.g., PLT-F4: 289.5–295.5) while plate baselines within the same orientation differ by up to 16 MPa (e.g., PLT-F4 vs PLT-F5: 305.5–311.5), so rows within a plate are strongly correlated and not independent.","observed_value":"within-plate range ~6 MPa; flat plate means span 292.5–308.5 MPa","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":14,"end_line":17,"quoted_text":"C013,PLT-F4,flat,A,289.5\nC014,PLT-F4,flat,B,291.5\nC015,PLT-F4,flat,C,293.5\nC016,PLT-F4,flat,D,295.5"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":18,"end_line":21,"quoted_text":"C017,PLT-F5,flat,A,305.5\nC018,PLT-F5,flat,B,307.5\nC019,PLT-F5,flat,C,309.5\nC020,PLT-F5,flat,D,311.5"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report is transparent about the clustering: it discloses that the file contains 12 distinct build plates alongside 48 coupon rows, so the row-level design is not concealed. This mitigates concerns of misrepresentation but does not make the row-independent test valid.","observed_value":"12 plates and 48 rows both reported","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":6,"end_line":7,"quoted_text":"- Coupon rows analysed: 48\n- Distinct build plates in file: 12"}]},{"description":"The effect is large enough that a correct plate-level analysis reaches the same qualitative conclusion: plate-mean groups (flat ≈ {296.5, 300.5, 304.5, 292.5, 308.5, 300.5}; edge ≈ {336.5, 340.5, 344.5, 332.5, 348.5, 340.5}, each SD ≈ 5.66) give t(10) ≈ 12.25, p < 0.0001. The demonstrated issue therefore invalidates the stated test statistic, df, and p-value, not the direction of the effect.","observed_value":"plate-level t(10) ≈ 12.25, still p < 0.0001","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"C001,PLT-F1,flat,A,293.5\nC002,PLT-F1,flat,B,295.5\nC003,PLT-F1,flat,C,297.5\nC004,PLT-F1,flat,D,299.5"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":26,"end_line":29,"quoted_text":"C025,PLT-E1,edge,A,332.5\nC026,PLT-E1,edge,B,335.5\nC027,PLT-E1,edge,C,337.5\nC028,PLT-E1,edge,D,340.5"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to defeat the finding three ways. (1) I searched the workflow for any aggregation to the declared independent unit before inference: uts_by_orientation iterates over raw rows and plate_id is only used to count distinct plates for display (workflow/analysis.py line 41); no per-plate averaging, mixed model, or cluster correction exists, so the test is genuinely row-level. (2) I checked whether rows might in fact be independent despite the declaration: the data contradict this — within-plate coupon values are tightly clustered (range ~6 MPa) while plate means within an orientation spread over ~16 MPa, indicating substantial intra-plate correlation, and build_orientation is constant within plate, so the between-group comparison has only 12 independent units. (3) I checked whether the reported numbers could correspond to a valid unit-level test: recomputing at the plate level gives t(10) ≈ 12.25, not the reported t(46) = 23.465, confirming the reported statistic comes from the pseudoreplicated row-level procedure. The qualitative conclusion survives correct analysis, but the stated statistical result does not, so the issue class is demonstrated and bounded accordingly.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}